---
// PostHog, loaded from the official snippet rather than the npm package.
//
// WHY THE SNIPPET. This is a static site with no client framework and no
// bundled runtime — the only JavaScript it ships is the copy buttons and the
// course progress in this layout. Adding posthog-js as a dependency would make
// analytics the largest thing in the build and put a package on the critical
// path of a site whose entire pitch is that it is plain readable pages. The
// snippet is ~1KB inline and fetches the rest asynchronously, so a slow or
// blocked PostHog costs nothing but the analytics.
//
// WHY is:inline. Astro would otherwise process, typecheck and bundle this
// script, and the snippet's minified body plus `window.posthog` fail that on
// both counts. It is upstream's code, shipped verbatim, on purpose.
//
// UNSET IS A WORKING STATE, LOUDLY IN DEV. With no PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY the site
// builds, boots and serves exactly as it always did — a fork, a local preview
// or a PR build must not need an analytics key. But "no key" and "key typo'd"
// look identical from the outside and both silently discard every event, so dev
// says so in the console rather than leaving it to be discovered in a month of
// empty graphs.
//
// The variable name matches nickdonohue-site's, deliberately: one name across
// the house means one `gh secret set` habit rather than a per-repo lookup.
const apiKey = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY;
const apiHost = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST ?? 'https://us.i.posthog.com';
const enabled = Boolean(apiKey);

if (!enabled && import.meta.env.DEV) {
  console.error(
    'PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY variable required by PostHog is missing or un-configured, ' +
      'this causes events to be silently missed. This error stops appearing once ' +
      'PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY is configured'
  );
}
